<div class="qul-docs">
  <h2>About surah metadata</h2>
  <p>
    QUL exports metadata for each Surah of the Quran in both <strong>JSON</strong> and <strong>SQLite</strong> formats.
  </p>

  <h3>Sample JSON</h3>
  <p>An example of the JSON structure:</p>
  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>JSON</span></div>
    <pre><code>{
  "1": {
    "id": 1,
    "name": "Al-Fātiĥah",
    "name_simple": "Al-Fatihah",
    "name_arabic": "الفاتحة",
    "revelation_order": 5,
    "revelation_place": "makkah",
    "verses_count": 7,
    "bismillah_pre": false
  }
}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>Field descriptions</h3>
  <div class="tools-table-overflow">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Field</th>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>id</td>
          <td>Integer</td>
          <td>Surah number (1–114)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>name</td>
          <td>String</td>
          <td>Transliterated Surah name with diacritic</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>name_simple</td>
          <td>String</td>
          <td>Transliterated name of the Surah (e.g., <code>Al-Fatihah</code>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>name_arabic</td>
          <td>String</td>
          <td>Arabic name of the Surah (e.g., <code style="direction: rtl">الفاتحة</code>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>revelation_order</td>
          <td>Integer</td>
          <td>Order in which the Surah was revealed historically</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>revelation_place</td>
          <td>String</td>
          <td><code>makkah</code> or <code>madinah</code> – based on location of revelation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>verses_count</td>
          <td>Integer</td>
          <td>Total number of verses in the Surah</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>bismillah_pre</td>
          <td>Boolean</td>
          <td><code>true</code> if the Surah starts with <em>Bismillah</em> and it should be recited; <code>false</code> otherwise</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>How to use this resource</h3>
  <p>
    Download the JSON or SQLite file from the download links on this page. The JSON file is one object:
    each key is the surah number as a string (<code>"1"</code> to <code>"114"</code>) and always equals the
    <code>id</code> field. The SQLite file has the same fields in the <code>chapters</code> table (surahs are called
    chapters there), with <code>bismillah_pre</code> stored as <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Build verse keys.</strong>
      Use <code>id</code> and <code>verses_count</code> to generate every <code>surah:ayah</code> key of a surah.
      These keys match <code>verse_key</code> in the other QUL resources.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sort and filter.</strong>
      Sort by <code>revelation_order</code> (1 to 114) to list surahs in the order of revelation.
      Filter on <code>revelation_place</code>, which is <code>"makkah"</code> or <code>"madinah"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check for Bismillah.</strong>
      If <code>bismillah_pre</code> is <code>true</code>, show the Bismillah before the first ayah. It is
      <code>false</code> for two surahs only: Al-Fatihah, where the Bismillah is the first ayah, and At-Tawbah,
      which has none.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>JavaScript</span></div>
    <pre><code>// Setup: unzip the download and serve quran-metadata-surah-name.json from your app (here at /data/)
const surahs = await fetch('/data/quran-metadata-surah-name.json').then((res) =&gt; res.json());

// Get a surah by number
const alBaqarah = surahs['2']; // { id: 2, name_simple: "Al-Baqarah", verses_count: 286, ... }

// Every verse key of a surah
const verseKeys = Array.from({ length: alBaqarah.verses_count }, (_, i) =&gt; `${alBaqarah.id}:${i + 1}`);

// Surahs in the order of revelation
const chronological = Object.values(surahs).sort((a, b) =&gt; a.revelation_order - b.revelation_order);</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>SQL</span></div>
    <pre><code>-- SQLite: surahs are stored in the chapters table
SELECT id, name_simple, verses_count
FROM chapters
WHERE revelation_place = 'madinah'
ORDER BY revelation_order;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    For a longer walkthrough, see
    <a href='/docs/tutorial-quran-metadata-end-to-end'>Tutorial 7: Quran Metadata End-to-End</a>.
  </p>
</div>
